                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><div>
<div>
<p>Biochemistry is a foundational discipline in the life sciences that explores the structure and function of biomolecules at the molecular level. As a compulsory core course for undergraduates in biology, medicine, and pharmacology-related fields, it provides an essential framework for understanding living systems.</p>
<p>The course begins with the structure and function of key macromolecules, such as proteins and nucleic acids, and extends to their integrated metabolism and regulatory mechanisms within organisms. While emphasizing a solid grasp of fundamental principles, the curriculum incorporates seminar-style discussions to bridge theory with real-world research. Through case-based learning, students will develop a coherent and systematic understanding of biochemistry, enabling them to connect this knowledge with related fields like pharmacology and pharmacokinetics, and to appreciate its applications in drug discovery.</p>
<p>Students will also learn to utilize various academic resources, read and analyze scientific literature, and communicate biochemical concepts with clarity and professionalism. The course is designed to equip students with a strong academic foundation for future research and advanced study in biology, medicine, and pharmacology.</p>
</div>
</div></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
